            <Documents>
              <Document id="manual-zh-v13" description="Vendor manual, Chinese, version 1.3, 49 pages. The only source carrying the terminal table (2.2.2), the terminal wiring drawing (2.2.1, page 4), the LED meanings, the holding register table (4.5) and the system parameters (4.6)." filename="ETH-MODBUS-IO8R-A_Handbuch_V1.3_zh.pdf" mime="application/pdf" type="manual" url="https://oss.amsamotion.com/uploads/20250521_174621_.pdf"/>
              <Document id="manual-es-v13" description="Machine translation of the Chinese manual into Spanish. Useful for reading, NOT authoritative: where it differs from the Chinese original, the original holds." filename="ETH-MODBUS-IO8R-A_Manual_V1.3_ES.pdf" mime="application/pdf" type="manual" url="https://www.amsamotion.es/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/ETH-MODBUS-IO8R-A-Data-Acquisition-Module-Product-Manual.pdf"/>
            </Documents>
            <Notes>
              <Note about="Modules/Module[4]" date="2026-09-06" kind="warning" source="section 4.5 &quot;Holding register addresses&quot; (保持寄存器地址)" sourceDoc="manual-zh-v13">The two analog outputs were declared on holding 0x00 and 0x01 in this description. That was wrong, and provably so: the vendor's register table marks 0x00-0x05 as reserved (预留) and puts the outputs on 0x06 (&quot;first analog output channel&quot;, 第一路模拟量输出, PLC 40007) and 0x07 (&quot;second channel&quot;, 第二路, PLC 40008), both with function codes 0x03 and 0x06. A write to 0x00/0x01 lands in reserved registers and moves nothing.

Measured the same day in the Ackervielfalt plant: TEN bindings were affected — wAO_1 and wAO_2 on five devices each (Pfirsich, Stachelbeere, Maulbeere, Maibeere, Johannisbeere). The thirty analog INPUTS are correct: 0x00-0x02 are the three voltage inputs, 0x03-0x05 the three current inputs, sorted by signal kind.

The project file carries the register addresses copied onto every single variable, so this correction does NOT reach them. Only once bindings point at this type's signal node through signalRef does one correction here act on all instances.</Note>
              <Note about="ServiceParameters" date="2026-09-06" kind="question" source="sections 4.5 and 4.6" sourceDoc="manual-zh-v13">OPEN: how this device's configuration space is addressed.

Section 4.6, &quot;System parameters (modified by the host tool)&quot; (系统参数(上位机修改)), lists sixteen system parameters on 0x00-0x0F: station number (0x02), COM1 parameters (0x03), frame end time (0x04), discrete input display mode (0x05), bus mode (0x08), IP segments (0x09/0x0A), MAC (0x0B-0x0D), COM2 parameters (0x0E), per-port serial mode (0x0F).

Those are the SAME addresses as in section 4.5, where 0x00-0x05 are reserved and 0x06/0x07 carry the analog outputs. They cannot be the same registers. The heading says &quot;modified by the host&quot;, and the manual repeatedly names a dedicated host tool (专用上位机) — a vendor-specific configuration program. Ports 9502/9503 are NOT it; they serve TCP-to-RTU forwarding.

What separates the two spaces — a different unit identifier, a different function code, or no Modbus access at all — is stated in neither section. This is why the ServiceParameters of this description carry NO BusMapping: guessing an address here would mean writing a station number into an analog output.

To resolve: manual sections 5.2.3 to 5.2.5, or a read probe on holding 0x02 of a real device — if it answers 1 (station number) rather than 0 (reserved), the question is answered.

PARTLY ANSWERED on 2026-09-07 for two of those parameters, and NOT through this space: the station number (1-32) and the COM2 baud rate are set by the two DIP switches on the front, not over Modbus at all. See the two notes on the DIP switches. That narrows the open question to the rest of section 4.6.</Note>
              <Note about="Drawing" date="2026-09-06" kind="question" source="section 2.2.1 (terminal wiring drawing, page 4), section 2.2.2 (terminal table), cover photo" sourceDoc="manual-zh-v13">WHAT IS DOCUMENTED HERE AND WHAT IS STILL GUESSED. Read this before you trust a position.

REVISED on 2026-09-07. Most of what this note called guessed is now documented: the manual's own wiring drawing (section 2.2.1, page 4) shows the whole front, and the cover photo shows the real device. The drawing was rebuilt against both.

DOCUMENTED, and therefore reliable:
- the housing outline, 128 x 80 mm, from the vendor's CAD archive
- WHICH terminal sits in WHICH row, in what order, and under WHICH NAME. The upper row carries 16: 4M I4 V4, 5M I5 V5, then 1L 0.0-0.3 2L 0.4-0.7. The lower row carries 21: I3 I2 I1 1M, V3 V2 V1 2M, 3M 0.0-0.7, A B, 0V 24+. This was an ASSUMPTION until the wiring drawing was read; the assumption happened to be right.
- the arrangement of the front: COM2 (DB9 female) and the RJ45 on the left, the Q0 LED row above and the I0 row below, PWR/RUN/COMM in the middle, the two DIP switches and the reset button on the right
- the DB9 pin numbering: five pins in the right column (5 at the top, 1 at the bottom), four in the left (9 at the top, 6 at the bottom)
- the RJ45 indicator LEDs: green at the top right of the jack, yellow at the bottom right
- the DIP switches: DIP1 has three switches, DIP2 has five. Their decoding stands in two notes of its own.
- the labelling: the type name at the top right of the face, AMSAMOTION at the bottom right (cover photo)
- the LED meanings: PWR steady when powered, RUN slow blink when normal, COMM two-colour — yellow on COM1 traffic, red on COM2, alternating when both communicate

NOT FROM THE VENDOR, although it stands on this drawing:
- the &quot;.0&quot; to &quot;.7&quot; channel labels under the two LED rows. They are the Ackervielfalt plant's house style. See the warning note on this — the device itself carries them without the dot.

STILL GUESSED, and marked as such:
- the exact spacing and width of the terminals within each strip — divided evenly here
- the exact position and size of every feature within the housing. The wiring drawing is a schematic, not a dimensioned drawing: it shows WHAT sits WHERE relative to everything else, not at which millimetre.

WHAT THIS MEANS: the drawing is usable for ASSIGNING signals — an id is an id, and an assignment survives a better drawing as long as the ids stay. It is now also usable for RECOGNISING the device and finding a terminal by name. It is NOT usable for measuring anything.

TO RESOLVE THE REST: the vendor's dimensioned drawing (2D DWG exists for this housing) or a photograph of the front with a scale.</Note>
              <Note about="Drawing" date="2026-09-07" kind="info" source="section 6.2.3 &quot;Setting station 1-32&quot; (设置 1~32 站号), table &quot;Station-address DIP to ETH-MODBUS module station number&quot; (站地址拨码对应ETH-MODBUS模块站号表)" sourceDoc="manual-zh-v13">DIP2, labelled &quot;station address&quot; (站地址). Five switches, numbered 1 to 5.

The code is binary with switch 1 as the LEAST significant bit, and the value is offset by one: station = 1 + sum of the switches that are ON, where switch 1 counts 1, switch 2 counts 2, switch 3 counts 4, switch 4 counts 8, switch 5 counts 16.

  all OFF                     -&gt; station 1   (factory state)
  1 ON                        -&gt; 2
  2 ON                        -&gt; 3
  1+2 ON                      -&gt; 4
  3 ON                        -&gt; 5
  1+2+3+4+5 ON                -&gt; 32

WHICH WAY IS ON: the manual states it on the picture beside the table — pushed toward the NUMBERS is off (关 = closed/off), pushed toward the &quot;ON&quot; legend is on (开 = open/on). The drawing shows the factory state: every lever in the OFF half of its window.

STATIONS 33 TO 126 ARE NOT SET HERE. They are only reachable through the vendor's configuration tool, and only while all five switches are ON (that is, from station 32). See section 6.2.3, &quot;Setting station 33-126&quot; (设置 33~126 站号).

A CHANGE DOES NOT TAKE EFFECT WHILE POWERED. The module must be off for at least 3 seconds before the new address is read.</Note>
              <Note about="Drawing" date="2026-09-07" kind="info" source="section 6.2.4 &quot;COM2 baud rate setting&quot; (COM2 的波特率设置), table &quot;Baud-rate DIP to COM2 baud rate&quot; (波特率拨码对应COM2波特率对应表)" sourceDoc="manual-zh-v13">DIP1, labelled &quot;baud rate&quot; (波特率). It sets the baud rate of COM2, and of COM2 only. Three switches, numbered 1 to 3.

The full table, copied from the manual — all eight combinations, in its order:

  1    2    3     baud
  OFF  OFF  OFF   9600     (factory state)
  ON   OFF  OFF   19200
  OFF  ON   OFF   38400
  ON   ON   OFF   57600
  OFF  OFF  ON    115200
  ON   OFF  ON    9600
  OFF  ON   ON    9600
  ON   ON   ON    4800

IT IS NOT A BINARY CODE, and reading it as one gets three of the eight wrong. Five combinations carry the five documented rates; two more fall back to 9600, and all-ON is 4800 — the lowest rate, not the highest. Whoever computes the rate from the switch positions instead of looking it up will be right five times out of eight, which is the worst kind of wrong.

COM1 IS NOT SET HERE. Its parameters — and everything about COM2 except the baud rate: data bits, stop bits, parity — come from the vendor's configuration tool. Section 6.2.4 says so in a table of its own: COM1 &quot;settable only from the host tool&quot; (仅支持上位机设置), COM2 baud rate &quot;settable only by DIP switch&quot; (仅支持拨码开关设置).

WHICH WAY IS ON: pushed toward the NUMBERS is off (关 = closed/off), toward the &quot;ON&quot; legend is on (开 = open/on). A change needs the module off for at least 3 seconds.</Note>
              <Note about="Drawing" date="2026-09-07" kind="warning">THE Q0 / I0 CHANNEL LABELS ON THIS DRAWING ARE OURS, NOT THE VENDOR'S.

They come from how the Ackervielfalt plant names its channels, not from the data sheet. Corrected here on 2026-09-07 after the drawing was rebuilt: the note on the drawing listed the front arrangement as documented, and the channel labelling slipped in under that heading. It does not belong there.

WHAT THE VENDOR'S OWN PAPERS SHOW, and they do not agree with each other:
- the wiring drawing (section 2.2.1, page 4) writes &quot;Q0&quot; and &quot;I0&quot; over the two LED rows, with &quot;.0 .1 … .7&quot; under the individual LEDs
- the product photo on the cover writes the same row labels but the channels as plain &quot;0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7&quot;, without the leading dot

WHERE THE TERMINALS ARE CONCERNED there is no such ambiguity: the terminal table (2.2.2) and the wiring drawing both name the digital terminals &quot;0.0&quot; to &quot;0.7&quot;, and that is what the two terminal strips on this drawing carry. Those labels ARE the vendor's.

WHY IT MATTERS: whoever reads a channel name off this face and puts it in a wiring list is copying our house style, not the vendor's marking on the real device. On the device the LED row reads without dots.</Note>
              <Note about="Modules" date="2026-09-07" kind="warning" source="section 2.2.2 (terminal table), section 2.2.1 (wiring drawing)" sourceDoc="manual-zh-v13">CORRECTED on 2026-09-07: all 24 pool variables carried a wrong 'function', and their names were this plant's, not the vendor's.

WHAT WAS WRONG WITH 'function'. Every one of the 24 said &quot;DI&quot; — the eight relay outputs, the six analog inputs and the two analog outputs included. Sixteen of twenty-four were provably wrong against the terminal table: the upper strip's 0.0-0.7 are digital OUTPUTS (数字量输出), V1-V3 and I1-I3 are analog INPUTS, and I4/V4 and I5/V5 are the two analog OUTPUTS. Now DI / DO / AI / AO, the convention the Weidmueller descriptions in this tree already use.

WHAT DID NOT HAVE TO BE CORRECTED, and that is worth saying: 'direction', 'iecType' and every single BusMapping were already right — discrete/fc2 for the inputs, coil/fc5 for the relays, input/fc4 for the analog inputs, holding 0x06 and 0x07 with fc6 for the analog outputs. The wrong 'function' sat beside a correct address the whole time.

WHAT WAS WRONG WITH THE NAMES. They read T_1..T_8 and V_1..V_8 — the Ackervielfalt plant's naming for its own signals, sitting in a device TYPE that five instances share. A type carries the DEVICE's designation; what a plant calls its signal belongs to the instance. Now I0_0..I0_7 and Q0_0..Q0_7 after the LED rows the vendor prints on the front, V1-V3 and I1-I3 after the terminals, AO1/AO2 for the two analog output channels.

The underscore is ours: the device marks them I0.0 and Q0.0, and a dot cannot stand in an IEC identifier. Everything before the dot is the vendor's.

THE SAME DEFECT STANDS IN THE SISTER FILES: eth-modbus-io5r.fdd has 10 pool variables, eth-modbus-io16r.fdd has 32, and in both every one says &quot;DI&quot;. They were made the same way and have not been checked against their manuals.</Note>
              <Note about="Drawing" date="2026-09-07" kind="info" source="section 2.2.3 &quot;COM2 pin definition (DB9 female)&quot; (COM2 口（DB9 母头）引脚定义)" sourceDoc="manual-zh-v13">COM2 pinout — the DB9 FEMALE connector on the front left.

  pin  function              name
   1   not connected (空)    NC
   2   ground (地)           GND
   3   485A                  A
   4   not connected         NC
   5   ground (地)           GND
   6   not connected         NC
   7   not connected         NC
   8   485B                  B
   9   not connected         NC

THREE PINS OF NINE CARRY ANYTHING: 3 is A, 8 is B, and 2 and 5 are both ground. The other five are empty — this is a DB9 shell used for a two-wire RS-485 line, not a serial port. Whoever wires it as an RS-232 DB9 (2 = RxD, 3 = TxD, 5 = GND) puts the transmit line on 485A and gets a bus that answers nothing.

THE SAME SIGNALS SIT ON THE TERMINAL STRIP as well: A and B on the lower row, next to 0V/24+. Those belong to COM1, not COM2 — see section 4.2. Two RS-485 lines, two places; the DB9 is COM2, the screw terminals are COM1.

BOTH GROUNDS ARE THE SAME NET (地). Pin 5 is the usual one; pin 2 exists so a cable made for a different device still finds ground.

WHY IT MATTERS FOR THE DRAWING: the nine pins on this face are drawn in the vendor's order — five in the right column (5 at the top, 1 at the bottom), four in the left (9 at the top, 6 at the bottom). Reading a pin off the picture and wiring it is only safe with this table beside it.</Note>
              <Note about="StatusVariables" date="2026-09-07" kind="warning">PWR, RUN AND COMM ON THIS DRAWING SHOW WHAT THE BRIDGE KNOWS, NOT THE LAMPS ON THE DEVICE.

The three status LEDs are now assigned — xPwr, xRun, bComm under StatusVariables, with their colours and blink written out as State entries. That makes them readable on screen. It does NOT make them a mirror of the front panel, and the difference matters:

NO REGISTER OF THIS DEVICE REPORTS ITS OWN LEDS. The register tables cover the IO (4.3-4.5) and the system parameters (4.6); the front lamps appear only in the terminal table, as a description of what a human sees. So nothing can be read that would say &quot;PWR is lit&quot;.

WHAT THE THREE VARIABLES REALLY CARRY, and it is the bridge's own view:
- xPwr  — the device answered on Modbus within the timeout
- xRun  — the last poll cycle completed without an exception
- bComm — bit 0 traffic on COM1, bit 1 on COM2, as the bridge counts it

WHERE THAT DIFFERS FROM THE DEVICE: a module whose 24V is present but whose network cable is unplugged has PWR lit and xPwr false. One that answers while its RUN lamp stands still — a state the manual does not describe — would show xRun true. And COM2's traffic is only known to the bridge if the bridge is the one talking on COM2; a third party on that line blinks the real lamp and moves nothing here.

WHAT THE COLOURS ARE FROM: the vendor's terminal table (2.2.2) — PWR steady while supplied, RUN slow blink while running, COMM yellow on COM1, red on COM2, alternating when both communicate. The States repeat that wording so the screen and the device agree on what a colour means, even though they do not always agree on WHEN.

TO MAKE THEM REAL: the bridge would have to publish these three, and section 4.6 would have to yield a way to read the device's own indicator state — which the open note on ServiceParameters says it does not, yet.</Note>
              <Note about="ServiceParameters/ServiceParameter" date="2026-09-07" kind="warning" source="sections 4.5, 4.6 and 6.2.3" sourceDoc="manual-zh-v13">THE ADDRESS ON 'slave_id' IS NOT IN THE MANUAL. Do not write to it.

It declares holding register 4096 (0x1000), count 1. That number appears in no section of the vendor manual:
- 4.5 (holding registers) covers 0x00 to 0x07: 0x00-0x05 reserved, 0x06 and 0x07 the two analog outputs. Nothing above.
- 4.6 (system parameters) lists sixteen entries on 0x00-0x0F, with the station number on 0x02 — a different space, and the open note beside this one says how it is addressed is unresolved.
- 6.2.3 says the station number 1-32 is set by the &quot;station address&quot; DIP switch, and 33-126 only through the vendor's configuration tool. Not over Modbus at all.

So 0x1000 is a guess wearing the clothes of a fact. It has a count, a range and a default value beside it, which makes it look checked.

WHAT A WRITE THERE WOULD DO is unknown. If the device ignores unmapped holding registers, nothing. If it does not, something else changes and nobody knows what — and the module's own station number would be the least of it.

THE ENTRY IS KEPT, not deleted, because the QUESTION it stands for is real: the station number IS readable somewhere, and this is the right place to declare it once that is known. What has to go is the address.

TO RESOLVE: a read probe on a real device — holding 0x02 and 0x1000, both. If 0x02 answers 1 on a factory module and 0x1000 answers an exception, the answer is in section 4.6 and this entry moves there. That probe also settles the older open question on the configuration space, which is why it is worth doing once, carefully, on a module that is not in service.</Note>
              <Note about="StatusVariables" date="2026-09-07" kind="warning" source="anvil-server/proto/plc_service.proto (DeviceStateProto, DeviceStatusEvent)">THE THREE STATUS VARIABLES ON THIS DEVICE ARE INVENTED. The real ones already exist elsewhere.

CORRECTED on 2026-09-07, same day, after measuring instead of assuming. The first version of this note warned that xRun's ten values are ordinals of a Rust enum with nothing holding the two sides together. That was too sharp: the numbers stand in the .proto as DeviceStateProto — a written contract, not a silent one.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY THE CASE. Device status is delivered today, and by a path that works end to end: anvild's status_collector broadcasts, gRPC StreamBusStatus streams a DeviceStatusEvent per change plus a heartbeat, the editor subscribes on every login and re-subscribes when the stream dies, and the result paints the traffic light in the project tree and fills the properties panel. Nothing of that is switched off.

DeviceStatusEvent carries: segment_id, device_id, state, consecutive_failures, error_count, last_error_code, last_error_msg, last_transition_at_ms, observed_at_ms, anvil_group.

SO xPwr, xRun AND bComm ARE A SECOND MECHANISM BESIDE A WORKING ONE:
- xRun is nothing other than DeviceStatusEvent.state, copied under another name
- xPwr would be derived from that same state, not measured separately
- bComm has no counterpart at all — COM1/COM2 traffic is carried nowhere

THE TEN STATE ENTRIES ON THE RUN LED STAY CORRECT, because their values are DeviceStateProto's and that is the contract. What is wrong is the NAME they hang on.

WHAT IS MISSING IS ONE LINK, not three: nothing carries a DeviceStatusEvent into the DRAWING. It reaches the tree and the properties panel; the device face never sees it.

DO NOT BUILD A SECOND PATH FOR IT. A tool that writes a value straight into the drawing would prove that the tool works and nothing else — the producer, the stream and the join would all be skipped. The way in is the one the properties panel already uses.

Tracked as issue #101 in forgeiec-studio, with the measurement.</Note>
            </Notes>
          </DeviceDescription>
          <DeviceDescription xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" id="721f7517-0e34-487d-9e50-4e26285c08b7" catalogRef="Amsamotion/ETH-MODBUS-IO5R" version="1.0" xsi:schemaLocation="https://forgeiec.io/v2/fdd forgeiec-v2-fdd.xsd">
            <DeviceIdentity>
              <VendorName>Amsamotion</VendorName>
              <ProductName>ETH-MODBUS-IO5R</ProductName>
              <ProductId>ETH-MODBUS-IO5R</ProductId>
              <Description>5DI 5DO Relay Modbus TCP/RTU IO module, DIN rail, opto-isolated inputs (DC 20-28V), relay outputs (2A/point, 10A total)</Description>
              <DeviceFamily>IO Module</DeviceFamily>
              <Series>ETH-Modbus</Series>
            </DeviceIdentity>
            <CommProfiles>
              <CommProfile protocol="modbustcp">
                <DefaultSlaveId>1</DefaultSlaveId>
                <DefaultPort>502</DefaultPort>
                <ByteOrder>big-endian</ByteOrder>
                <WordOrder>big-endian</WordOrder>
              </CommProfile>
              <CommProfile protocol="modbusrtu">
                <DefaultSlaveId>1</DefaultSlaveId>
                <ByteOrder>big-endian</ByteOrder>
                <WordOrder>big-endian</WordOrder>
              </CommProfile>
            </CommProfiles>
            <Modules>
              <Module name="Digital Inputs" id="digital_inputs">
                <PoolVariable name="T_1" id="di_1" description="Digital input 1 (opto-isolated, DC 20-28V)" direction="in" function="DI" iecType="BOOL">
                  <BusMapping address="0" count="1" functionCode="2" registerType="discrete"/>
                </PoolVariable>
                <PoolVariable name="T_2" id="di_2" description="Digital input 2 (opto-isolated, DC 20-28V)" direction="in" function="DI" iecType="BOOL">
                  <BusMapping address="1" count="1" functionCode="2" registerType="discrete"/>
                </PoolVariable>
                <PoolVariable name="T_3" id="di_3" description="Digital input 3 (opto-isolated, DC 20-28V)" direction="in" function="DI" iecType="BOOL">
                  <BusMapping address="2" count="1" functionCode="2" registerType="discrete"/>
                </PoolVariable>
                <PoolVariable name="T_4" id="di_4" description="Digital input 4 (opto-isolated, DC 20-28V)" direction="in" function="DI" iecType="BOOL">
                  <BusMapping address="3" count="1" functionCode="2" registerType="discrete"/>
                </PoolVariable>
                <PoolVariable name="T_5" id="di_5" description="Digital input 5 (opto-isolated, DC 20-28V)" direction="in" function="DI" iecType="BOOL">
                  <BusMapping address="4" count="1" functionCode="2" registerType="discrete"/>
                </PoolVariable>
              </Module>
              <Module name="Relay Outputs" id="relay_outputs">
                <PoolVariable name="V_1" id="do_1" description="Relay output 1 (2A max per point)" direction="out" function="DI" iecType="BOOL">
                  <BusMapping address="0" count="1" functionCode="5" registerType="coil"/>
                </PoolVariable>
                <PoolVariable name="V_2" id="do_2" description="Relay output 2 (2A max per point)" direction="out" function="DI" iecType="BOOL">
                  <BusMapping address="1" count="1" functionCode="5" registerType="coil"/>
                </PoolVariable>
                <PoolVariable name="V_3" id="do_3" description="Relay output 3 (2A max per point)" direction="out" function="DI" iecType="BOOL">
                  <BusMapping address="2" count="1" functionCode="5" registerType="coil"/>
                </PoolVariable>
                <PoolVariable name="V_4" id="do_4" description="Relay output 4 (2A max per point)" direction="out" function="DI" iecType="BOOL">
                  <BusMapping address="3" count="1" functionCode="5" registerType="coil"/>
                </PoolVariable>
                <PoolVariable name="V_5" id="do_5" description="Relay output 5 (2A max per point)" direction="out" function="DI" iecType="BOOL">
                  <BusMapping address="4" count="1" functionCode="5" registerType="coil"/>
                </PoolVariable>
              </Module>
              <!-- Relay readback removed: coil status is implicitly read by the bridge -->
            </Modules>
            <ServiceParameters>
              <ServiceParameter name="SlaveId" id="slave_id" accessRights="rw" description="Modbus station number" iecType="UINT">
                <DefaultValue>1</DefaultValue>
                <Range max="247" min="1"/>
              </ServiceParameter>
              <ServiceParameter name="BaudRate" id="baud_rate" accessRights="rw" description="Serial baud rate (RTU mode)" iecType="UDINT">
                <DefaultValue>115200</DefaultValue>
                <EnumValues>[{&quot;value&quot;:9600,&quot;label&quot;:&quot;9600&quot;},{&quot;value&quot;:19200,&quot;label&quot;:&quot;19200&quot;},{&quot;value&quot;:38400,&quot;label&quot;:&quot;38400&quot;},{&quot;value&quot;:57600,&quot;label&quot;:&quot;57600&quot;},{&quot;value&quot;:115200,&quot;label&quot;:&quot;115200&quot;}]</EnumValues>
              </ServiceParameter>
            </ServiceParameters>
            <StatusVariables>
              <StatusVariable name="wModbusRetries" description="Number of Modbus frame retransmissions since tongs started." iecType="UINT"/>
              <StatusVariable name="wModbusExceptionCount" description="Number of Modbus exception responses (illegal function / data / ...)." iecType="UINT"/>
              <StatusVariable name="xPwr" description="PWR as the bridge sees it: the device answered on Modbus within the timeout. NOT the mains lamp on the front — no register of this device reports its own LEDs (manual chapter 5 lists no such register)." iecType="BOOL"/>
              <StatusVariable name="xSys" description="SYS as the bridge sees it: the DeviceState the connector publishes, 0 to 9, the ordinals of tongs-core's DeviceState enum. On the device SYS blinks at 0.5 s while the system is healthy (manual 3.2) — that blink is NOT what this variable carries." iecType="USINT"/>
            </StatusVariables>
